{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses spoke to the children of Israel</b> — to interrupt the matter, the words of Rabbi Yishmael. For until here the words are from the Omnipresent, and the section of vows begins with Moses' speech — it was necessary to interrupt the matter, to say that Moses went back and said this section to Israel, for otherwise it could be understood that he did not say this to them but began his words with the section of vows. This is the language of Rashi. But I do not understand this, for thus it is said, <b>\"And Moses spoke to Aaron and to his sons and to all the children of Israel\"</b> (Leviticus 21:24) at the end of the section of blemishes, and at the end of the section of the appointed seasons it says, <b>\"And Moses declared the appointed seasons of HaShem to the children of Israel\"</b> (Leviticus 23:44). But according to its plain sense, Scripture comes to say that even though this section is in the law of offerings, He did not single out Aaron and his sons in it as He did in the sections that are in the Torah of the Priests, but rather spoke to all the children of Israel together, <b>\"according to all that HaShem commanded Moses\"</b> — both in the matter of ceasing from labor, and in the daily and additional offerings, and in vows and freewill offerings, for thus He was told, <b>\"Command the children of Israel\"</b> (Numbers 28:2). And the reason is because the commandment applies after their entry into the land, and it is a warning to all Israel that they observe in their seasons the daily and additional offerings and bring vows and freewill offerings. Furthermore, the essence of the commandment is that the days be recalled and observed as days of cessation from all labor of divine service. And this section parallels the section of the appointed seasons in the Torah of the Priests (Leviticus 23:2). There it says at the beginning, <b>\"Speak to the children of Israel and say to them,\"</b> and at the end, <b>\"And Moses declared the appointed seasons of HaShem,\"</b> and so here: at the beginning, <b>\"Command the children of Israel and say to them,\"</b> and at the end, <b>\"And Moses spoke to the children of Israel according to all that HaShem commanded.\"</b> And He did not mention <b>\"the appointed seasons of HaShem\"</b> because other matters that are not among the appointed seasons of HaShem are mentioned here in the section, such as the daily offerings of weekdays, the additional offerings of the Sabbath, and the new moons. And the midrash that Rabbi Yishmael derived from it — since Scripture does not need to say about Moses that he said to the children of Israel all that HaShem commanded him, and it is not the way of the Torah to say so in every single section — therefore they expound in <b>\"And Moses spoke to Aaron and to his sons and to all the children of Israel\"</b>: he warned Aaron through the sons, and the sons through Israel, and Israel one through another (Sifra, Emor 3:12). And in the section of the appointed seasons they made many midrashim: it teaches that Moses would say to Israel the laws of Pesach at Pesach, the laws of Atzeret at Atzeret, the laws of the Festival at the Festival; in the language that he heard them he would say them to Israel — all the sections were said in one manner. Rabbi Yosi HaGelili says: <b>\"The appointed seasons of HaShem\"</b> are stated, but the Sabbath of Creation is not stated with them. Ben Azzai says: <b>\"The appointed seasons of HaShem\"</b> are stated, but the section of vows is not stated with them. Rabbi says: What is the reason to say <b>\"And Moses declared the appointed seasons of HaShem to the children of Israel\"</b>? For we have learned only about the Pesach and the daily offering that they override the Sabbath, since it is stated about them <b>\"in its appointed season,\"</b> but other communal offerings we have not learned. It was necessary to say: <b>\"These you shall offer to HaShem at your appointed seasons\"</b> — the omer and its accompanying offering, the two loaves and their accompanying offering we have not heard about, and when it says <b>\"And Moses declared the appointed seasons of HaShem to the children of Israel\"</b> — he established an appointed season for all of them. Thus far in the Torah of the Priests (Sifra, Emor 17:12–13). And it is possible to say that the midrash of Rabbi Yishmael, who said to interrupt the matter, is the same as the midrash of Ben Azzai, who said: <b>\"The appointed seasons of HaShem\"</b> are stated, but the section of vows is not stated with them — to say that the law of the appointed seasons is not like the law of the section of vows, which was said to the heads of the tribes, to say that a single expert may annul a vow, and likewise three laymen, but the appointed seasons require sanctification by a court of three experts, as is explained in Bava Batra (121b). Ben Azzai derived this distinction between them from the limitation <b>\"of HaShem's appointed seasons,\"</b> and Rabbi Yishmael derived from the extra verse that comes only to interrupt the matter of the appointed seasons from the matter of vows — both of them said one and the same thing, yet there is a difference in exegetical implication between them. And the correct view in my eyes is that his intent is only to say that if he had completed the section of the appointed seasons with <b>\"These you shall offer to HaShem at your appointed seasons,\"</b> and then begun <b>\"And Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes of the children of Israel, saying: This is the matter that HaShem commanded,\"</b> it would have been possible for Scripture to refer back above, to say that Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes <b>\"This is the matter that HaShem commanded\"</b> regarding the daily and additional offerings mentioned, and <b>\"to the heads of the tribes\"</b> would not be expounded in connection with the section of vows that follows. Therefore he interrupted and concluded the section of the appointed seasons with <b>\"And Moses spoke to the children of Israel according to all that HaShem commanded Moses,\"</b> and began the section of vows with <b>\"And Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes: This is the matter that HaShem commanded.\"</b> And this is the truth, for thus the meaning of <b>\"to interrupt the matter\"</b> is explained in many places in Sifra and Sifrei. And Rashi already cited (Rashi on Numbers 31:17) what they said there in Sifrei (Matot 31): <b>\"Slay\"</b> — why is it said? To interrupt the matter, the words of Rabbi Yishmael. For if I read <b>\"Slay every male among the little ones, and every woman who has known a man by lying with him... and all the little ones among the women\"</b> — I do not know in what matter Scripture is speaking, therefore <b>\"Slay\"</b> is stated to interrupt the matter, the words of Rabbi Yishmael. And likewise in other places in the Torah of the Priests:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "The abbreviations תו״כ and ר׳ in the Hebrew have been expanded to their full forms (Sifra/Torat Kohanim and Rabbi respectively) following standard convention. The abbreviation ה' has been rendered HaShem throughout, including within quoted verses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:51.252905640Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}